WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.134 --> 00:00:02.802 align:start size:88% position:13%
- I think there's a
kind of a misconception

00:00:02.802 --> 00:00:04.738 align:start size:84% position:16%
that Bitcoin is
completely anonymous.

00:00:04.738 --> 00:00:06.940
It's really pseudo anonymous.

00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:09.976 align:start size:84% position:16%
If a state actor or
even a big corporation

00:00:09.976 --> 00:00:13.279 align:start size:97% position:3%
wants to find out who
transferred Bitcoin to whom

00:00:13.279 --> 00:00:15.281
it can be done.

00:00:15.281 --> 00:00:19.452 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's harder than just
looking at someone's Visa

00:00:19.452 --> 00:00:22.022
or bank account but, I think,

00:00:22.022 --> 00:00:24.324
there are new technologies
built on top of Bitcoin,

00:00:24.324 --> 00:00:25.825
like the lightning network,

00:00:25.825 --> 00:00:28.995
that for very small amounts
will keep it almost like cash

00:00:28.995 --> 00:00:32.732
where it will be much harder
and won't be worth the effort.

00:00:32.732 --> 00:00:35.402
(intense music)

00:00:39.906 --> 00:00:41.041 line:0%
- All right, I'm Dave Rubin.

00:00:41.041 --> 00:00:43.643 line:0%
We are on location in Miami.

00:00:43.643 --> 00:00:45.578 line:0%
I am with Alex Lloyd,

00:00:45.578 --> 00:00:48.715 align:start size:84% position:16%
co author of The
Little Bitcoin Book:

00:00:48.715 --> 00:00:53.420
Why Bitcoin Matters For Your
Freedom, Finances, and Future.

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:54.921
We're obviously in the
right city for that,

00:00:54.921 --> 00:00:57.290
so Lloyd, why does it matter?

00:00:57.290 --> 00:01:00.193 line:0%
- You know we came at this book

00:01:00.193 --> 00:01:01.628 line:0%
from a human rights perspective.

00:01:01.628 --> 00:01:04.130 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
One of my co-authors,
Alex Gladstein,

00:01:04.130 --> 00:01:05.632
is the Chief Strategy Officer

00:01:05.632 --> 00:01:07.333
of the Human Rights Foundation

00:01:07.333 --> 00:01:09.803
and there were a lot
of books out there

00:01:09.803 --> 00:01:12.205
about the technical nature
and the financial nature

00:01:12.205 --> 00:01:13.740
of why Bitcoin's important.

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:17.644
This one really gives more
a sociopolitical aspect,

00:01:17.644 --> 00:01:20.880
why everyday people,

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:22.582
Bitcoin is going to
be important to them

00:01:22.582 --> 00:01:24.117
and that's kind of
the angle we took.

00:01:24.117 --> 00:01:25.485 align:start size:84% position:16%
- So, we talked about
this a little bit

00:01:25.485 --> 00:01:26.886
at dinner last night, I mean,

00:01:26.886 --> 00:01:30.857 align:start size:88% position:13%
basically you think
Bitcoin will free people

00:01:30.857 --> 00:01:33.760
as these systems
around us go down.

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:35.261
Is that fair to say?

00:01:35.261 --> 00:01:38.932
- I think what it is is it's
a democratization of capital.

00:01:38.932 --> 00:01:40.200
If you think of the history

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:43.903
of how individuals got
more power in the world,

00:01:43.903 --> 00:01:45.071
it started with democracy.

00:01:45.071 --> 00:01:46.372
There's a lot of steps here,

00:01:46.372 --> 00:01:48.975
but democracy as a
political system.

00:01:48.975 --> 00:01:51.778
I think, another step
that you might think of

00:01:51.778 --> 00:01:54.714 align:start size:84% position:16%
is freedom of speech
and the internet,

00:01:54.714 --> 00:01:57.617
which let anybody be a
publisher and a creator

00:01:57.617 --> 00:01:59.586
and I think Bitcoin
is the next step,

00:01:59.586 --> 00:02:02.288
which is people can
be their own banks

00:02:02.288 --> 00:02:04.924
and have their own
individual freedom.

00:02:04.924 --> 00:02:07.193
Whereas the internet,
in a way, was a way

00:02:07.193 --> 00:02:10.530 align:start size:97% position:3%
to let any two people exchange
ideas and information,

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:12.499
and Bitcoin is going to be a way

00:02:12.499 --> 00:02:15.101
that any two people
can exchange value

00:02:15.101 --> 00:02:17.871
and that's a big
part of this book.

00:02:17.871 --> 00:02:20.206 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Since most of my audience
is pretty tech savvy,

00:02:20.206 --> 00:02:21.441
but not everybody,

00:02:21.441 --> 00:02:22.675
for the person that's
watching this right now

00:02:22.675 --> 00:02:24.210
that has no Bitcoin.

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:25.712
They've heard the word Bitcoin,

00:02:25.712 --> 00:02:27.113
they're like, what
the hell is Bitcoin?

00:02:27.113 --> 00:02:29.315 align:start size:97% position:3%
It's not backed by anything,
what is this thing?

00:02:29.315 --> 00:02:31.384 align:start size:84% position:16%
Give me me the Q and
A portion of this

00:02:31.384 --> 00:02:32.619
'cause you guys end this,

00:02:32.619 --> 00:02:34.888
chapter six is all a Q
and A about the basics.

00:02:34.888 --> 00:02:38.758 align:start size:84% position:16%
- I think if you think
of, as Americans,

00:02:38.758 --> 00:02:41.461 align:start position:0%
a lot of people don't
understand the privilege we have

00:02:41.461 --> 00:02:45.398
in terms of the amount
of financial resources,

00:02:45.398 --> 00:02:48.735 align:start size:97% position:3%
banks, credit card, Apple
Pay, all these systems that,

00:02:50.103 --> 00:02:52.405 align:start size:97% position:3%
PayPal, that let us exchange
value between people

00:02:52.405 --> 00:02:54.741 align:start size:84% position:16%
but billions of people
across the world

00:02:54.741 --> 00:02:56.142
don't have any such technology.

00:02:56.142 --> 00:02:58.344 align:start size:88% position:13%
They probably will never
have a bank account

00:02:58.344 --> 00:03:01.181 align:start position:0%
and Bitcoin is way for them
to, in a way, be their own bank

00:03:01.181 --> 00:03:03.783
and in an unstoppable way

00:03:03.783 --> 00:03:06.719 align:start size:97% position:3%
exchange value with anybody
across the whole world

00:03:06.719 --> 00:03:09.122
and, I think, that's the message

00:03:09.122 --> 00:03:14.127
that really makes Bitcoin not
this crazy trading scheme.

00:03:16.095 --> 00:03:20.633 line:0%
A lot of people think it's
just like a Ponzi scheme

00:03:20.633 --> 00:03:24.070 align:start position:0%
but this is, if you think about
it, it's a way for people,

00:03:24.070 --> 00:03:27.807 align:start size:88% position:13%
billions of people to
exchange and store value

00:03:27.807 --> 00:03:29.976
without having any
government or company

00:03:29.976 --> 00:03:32.078 align:start size:94% position:6%
involved in it and that's
a very powerful idea.

00:03:32.078 --> 00:03:35.014
- What about the other cryptos,
where do they fit into this?

00:03:35.014 --> 00:03:36.249
The other coins?

00:03:36.249 --> 00:03:37.784
- This book is really
focused on Bitcoin.

00:03:37.784 --> 00:03:39.018
I think there's a place

00:03:39.018 --> 00:03:40.353
for a lot of other
types of technology

00:03:40.353 --> 00:03:41.888
and Bitcoin is an
evolving thing,

00:03:41.888 --> 00:03:44.591
there's going to be other
things that graft onto it

00:03:44.591 --> 00:03:47.927
like the lightning network,
so, you'll be able to do

00:03:47.927 --> 00:03:50.630
millions or billions
of transactions a day.

00:03:50.630 --> 00:03:55.034
But Bitcoin in and of itself
is really the preeminent way

00:03:55.034 --> 00:03:56.603
to store value in an exchange.

00:03:56.603 --> 00:04:00.540
It was really designed
in an amazingly smart way

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:02.508 align:start size:84% position:16%
and the book goes into
why it's different

00:04:02.508 --> 00:04:05.745
from any previous type of money.

00:04:05.745 --> 00:04:06.980
- Can you explain the difference

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:08.748
between why you would
use Bitcoin to store

00:04:08.748 --> 00:04:10.216
versus necessarily using it

00:04:10.216 --> 00:04:12.852
as what you're buying with,
basically, all the time?

00:04:12.852 --> 00:04:14.988 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Well, I think that
as it stands now,

00:04:14.988 --> 00:04:17.156 align:start size:94% position:6%
Bitcoin is probably not
the best way to buy things

00:04:17.156 --> 00:04:20.293
just because there's
millions of other,

00:04:20.293 --> 00:04:21.894
easier ways to exchange value.

00:04:21.894 --> 00:04:24.430 align:start size:88% position:13%
If you're in the United
States or Europe,

00:04:25.732 --> 00:04:27.867
a company that has
a banking system,

00:04:27.867 --> 00:04:29.335
but what we've seen

00:04:29.335 --> 00:04:34.173 align:start size:97% position:3%
is there are human rights
advocates throughout the world

00:04:35.541 --> 00:04:38.478 align:start size:94% position:6%
who live in dictatorships
and they have no way

00:04:38.478 --> 00:04:40.413 align:start size:94% position:6%
of safely storing
anything that they create

00:04:40.413 --> 00:04:41.681
in terms of value.

00:04:41.681 --> 00:04:45.118
And if they store cash
under their mattress,

00:04:45.118 --> 00:04:46.352
that can get taken,

00:04:46.352 --> 00:04:47.987
if they have money in
their bank account,

00:04:47.987 --> 00:04:51.624
that could get taken by
the government by fear

00:04:53.026 --> 00:04:54.260
but, what's even worse,

00:04:54.260 --> 00:04:56.195
a lot of countries
have hyperinflation,

00:04:56.195 --> 00:04:57.997
so that's just an insidious way

00:04:57.997 --> 00:04:59.499
of stealing money from people

00:04:59.499 --> 00:05:03.036 align:start size:97% position:3%
without them really noticing
it in the short term.

00:05:03.036 --> 00:05:04.570
- That's kind of where
we're going right now.

00:05:04.570 --> 00:05:08.441 align:start position:0%
- It's possible and that's
maybe a bigger case for Bitcoin.

00:05:08.441 --> 00:05:12.378 align:start size:88% position:13%
But Bitcoin is the first
time that people...

00:05:12.378 --> 00:05:15.448 align:start size:97% position:3%
The Human Rights
Foundation does give grants

00:05:15.448 --> 00:05:18.651 align:start size:94% position:6%
to human rights advocates
throughout the world

00:05:18.651 --> 00:05:21.521 align:start size:97% position:3%
and before it was a multi-step
process that took days

00:05:21.521 --> 00:05:26.526
and was fraught with peril in
terms of getting them capital.

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:30.363
And now, through Bitcoin,
there's really a simple way

00:05:30.363 --> 00:05:34.434
that we can send people capital

00:05:34.434 --> 00:05:36.903
and they can use it and
store it in a safe way

00:05:36.903 --> 00:05:38.671
and that's really never
been possible before.

00:05:38.671 --> 00:05:40.239
- Right, so basically,
Human Rights Foundation,

00:05:40.239 --> 00:05:41.474
you can find people

00:05:41.474 --> 00:05:43.543
that are doing good
work in some country,

00:05:43.543 --> 00:05:45.912
trying to free people,
whatever, whatever it is

00:05:45.912 --> 00:05:47.146
and then you can
send them Bitcoin

00:05:47.146 --> 00:05:48.981
and that way whatever
work they're doing,

00:05:48.981 --> 00:05:51.117 align:start size:94% position:6%
that the government is not
probably thrilled with,

00:05:51.117 --> 00:05:53.186 align:start size:88% position:13%
it's theirs and the
government, technically,

00:05:53.186 --> 00:05:54.020
it doesn't even know about it.

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:55.488
- Exactly.

00:05:55.488 --> 00:05:56.989
- Should we be talking
about that right now, or?

00:05:56.989 --> 00:05:58.791 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Like what, the government
not knowing about it?

00:05:58.791 --> 00:06:01.494
I think there's a misconception

00:06:01.494 --> 00:06:03.463 align:start size:84% position:16%
that Bitcoin is
completely anonymous.

00:06:03.463 --> 00:06:05.665
It's really pseudo anonymous.

00:06:05.665 --> 00:06:08.668 align:start size:84% position:16%
If a state actor or
even a big corporation

00:06:08.668 --> 00:06:12.605 align:start size:97% position:3%
wants to find out who
transferred Bitcoin to whom

00:06:12.605 --> 00:06:13.473
it can be done.

00:06:15.308 --> 00:06:18.177 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's harder than just
looking at someone's visa

00:06:18.177 --> 00:06:20.747
or bank account but, I think,

00:06:20.747 --> 00:06:23.449
there are new technologies
built on top of Bitcoin,

00:06:23.449 --> 00:06:24.951
like the lightning network,

00:06:24.951 --> 00:06:27.687
that for very small amounts
will keep it almost like cash

00:06:27.687 --> 00:06:28.921
where it'll be much harder

00:06:28.921 --> 00:06:31.924
and won't be worth
the effort to track.

00:06:31.924 --> 00:06:33.059
- How's this all connected

00:06:33.059 --> 00:06:35.061 align:start size:84% position:16%
to just decentralizing
everything?

00:06:35.061 --> 00:06:37.397
You know, the world I'm in
where everybody's worried

00:06:37.397 --> 00:06:40.233
that if you put a video up
that YouTube doesn't like

00:06:40.233 --> 00:06:41.734
you're going down.

00:06:41.734 --> 00:06:43.035
Or if you put a Tweet up
that Twitter doesn't like,

00:06:43.035 --> 00:06:44.203
they're going to get rid of you.

00:06:44.203 --> 00:06:45.571 align:start size:84% position:16%
Just the general
decentralization idea.

00:06:45.571 --> 00:06:47.407
- I think this was the proof,

00:06:47.407 --> 00:06:49.909
the first technology that
really proved it could work

00:06:49.909 --> 00:06:54.247
and the designer or inventor
of Bitcoin was really smart

00:06:54.247 --> 00:06:58.918
in terms of creating an
amazing incentive network

00:06:58.918 --> 00:07:01.454
where hundreds of
millions of dollars

00:07:01.454 --> 00:07:04.991 align:start size:88% position:13%
has been invested in the
blockchain technology

00:07:04.991 --> 00:07:08.761 align:start size:97% position:3%
and the mining around it
without really any investment

00:07:08.761 --> 00:07:11.564
from government or
venture capitalists.

00:07:11.564 --> 00:07:12.832
- Can you give me mining 101

00:07:12.832 --> 00:07:14.634 align:start size:84% position:16%
for people that
don't get it at all?

00:07:15.501 --> 00:07:17.203
- Bitcoin is all about proving

00:07:17.203 --> 00:07:21.107 align:start size:94% position:6%
that what's in the
public ledger is accurate

00:07:21.107 --> 00:07:23.409
and there's two parts of that.

00:07:23.409 --> 00:07:24.744
One is basically proving

00:07:24.744 --> 00:07:27.880 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
that having thousands
of independent computers

00:07:29.148 --> 00:07:30.650 line:0%
and people running
those computers,

00:07:30.650 --> 00:07:32.452 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
check to see that that
ledger is correct.

00:07:32.452 --> 00:07:34.787
And then the other is
finding new Bitcoin

00:07:34.787 --> 00:07:37.156
and that's proving
the work that you did

00:07:37.156 --> 00:07:40.793
enables the incentive
structure to be set up

00:07:40.793 --> 00:07:43.663
so that Bitcoin can be created.

00:07:43.663 --> 00:07:47.600 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Right, what about when Elon
Musk is like, I'm into it

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:49.702 align:start position:0%
and it goes like this, and
then he's like, I'm not into it

00:07:49.702 --> 00:07:50.870
and it goes like this

00:07:50.870 --> 00:07:52.438 align:start size:88% position:13%
and then that gets
people all freaked out.

00:07:52.438 --> 00:07:56.042
- I feel like he's having fun.

00:07:56.042 --> 00:07:57.577
He's a very smart guy,

00:07:57.577 --> 00:08:00.580
he's created some really
incredible technologies

00:08:00.580 --> 00:08:01.781
but he's also someone

00:08:01.781 --> 00:08:02.915 align:start size:81% position:19%
who probably likes
to troll a lot

00:08:02.915 --> 00:08:06.285 align:start size:88% position:13%
on Twitter.
- You think, you think?

00:08:06.285 --> 00:08:11.290
- And he also runs a company
that is very focused on green,

00:08:13.092 --> 00:08:16.262
which is Tesla and I'm
sure he got pushed back

00:08:16.262 --> 00:08:18.397 align:start size:94% position:6%
saying Bitcoin isn't
green, which I think it is

00:08:18.397 --> 00:08:21.701
because a majority,
70% of the Bitcoin,

00:08:21.701 --> 00:08:23.503
is mined from renewable sources

00:08:23.503 --> 00:08:25.905 align:start position:0%
because people are really
looking for the cheapest energy

00:08:25.905 --> 00:08:27.139
when they mine a Bitcoin

00:08:27.139 --> 00:08:28.708 align:start size:84% position:16%
and that's, typically,
renewable stuff.

00:08:28.708 --> 00:08:31.077
- Right, but can you
power it by volcanoes?

00:08:31.077 --> 00:08:33.045 align:start size:88% position:13%
Isn't that what the
President of El Salvador

00:08:33.045 --> 00:08:33.946
is trying to do?
- We will see.

00:08:33.946 --> 00:08:35.414 align:start size:75% position:25%
I hope it works.
- Is that

00:08:35.414 --> 00:08:36.883
literally what he's trying
to do, it seemed like a joke?

00:08:36.883 --> 00:08:40.119 align:start position:0%
- Well, if you think about where
Bitcoin mining takes place,

00:08:40.119 --> 00:08:44.257
it needs a power source and if
you create a huge solar cell

00:08:44.257 --> 00:08:46.826
in the middle of
the Sahara Desert,

00:08:46.826 --> 00:08:48.394
it's not really valuable energy

00:08:48.394 --> 00:08:52.164
because you have to then
ship that energy to a city.

00:08:52.164 --> 00:08:55.701
But if you created a
huge green solar plant

00:08:55.701 --> 00:08:57.303
in the middle of
this Sahara Desert

00:08:57.303 --> 00:08:59.539
and your output was new Bitcoin,

00:08:59.539 --> 00:09:03.576 align:start size:97% position:3%
that might be a financially
viable situations

00:09:03.576 --> 00:09:05.444
because you can just email out

00:09:05.444 --> 00:09:06.946
the new Bitcoin that you create

00:09:06.946 --> 00:09:10.016
and the output is not a
huge cable pumping power,

00:09:10.016 --> 00:09:11.450
it's just some numbers.

00:09:11.450 --> 00:09:13.853 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Right, for somebody
watching this, that's like,

00:09:13.853 --> 00:09:15.988 align:start size:88% position:13%
all right, I didn't know
much about it before

00:09:15.988 --> 00:09:17.957
I got some basics here.

00:09:17.957 --> 00:09:19.592 align:start size:84% position:16%
Where do they start
with all this?

00:09:19.592 --> 00:09:22.995 align:start size:97% position:3%
Do you send them to a wallet
to start, an exchange,

00:09:22.995 --> 00:09:24.363
where do you send people?

00:09:24.363 --> 00:09:26.465 align:start size:97% position:3%
- There's a lot of
different ways and, I think,

00:09:28.901 --> 00:09:30.836 align:start size:94% position:6%
everybody is going to
have a different approach.

00:09:30.836 --> 00:09:35.541 align:start size:97% position:3%
You can go to a centrally
stored wallet, like Coinbase.

00:09:35.541 --> 00:09:37.610 align:start size:84% position:16%
You can also have
an app on your phone

00:09:37.610 --> 00:09:39.378
where you store stuff.

00:09:39.378 --> 00:09:42.048 align:start size:94% position:6%
You could have a USB stick
where you store stuff.

00:09:42.048 --> 00:09:44.584
And I think the
major difference,

00:09:44.584 --> 00:09:46.652 align:start position:0%
and maybe a lot of people don't
wrap their head around it,

00:09:46.652 --> 00:09:47.887
is that you're your own bank,

00:09:47.887 --> 00:09:49.689
so that gives you
a lot of freedom

00:09:49.689 --> 00:09:51.457
but then it gives you
some responsibilities

00:09:51.457 --> 00:09:54.894
because you have
to be the person

00:09:54.894 --> 00:09:57.196
who creates the wall
around your privacy

00:09:57.196 --> 00:10:00.967
and you have responsibilities
when you get that freedom.

00:10:00.967 --> 00:10:02.802 align:start size:84% position:16%
- So that stick that
I have in a safe

00:10:02.802 --> 00:10:04.604 align:start size:97% position:3%
and the password and all that,
I can't lose that, right?

00:10:04.604 --> 00:10:05.538
- You can't lose that.

00:10:05.538 --> 00:10:06.672
- That's the key to this thing,

00:10:06.672 --> 00:10:08.007 align:start size:88% position:13%
don't lose the password?
- And you don't

00:10:08.007 --> 00:10:09.508 align:start size:97% position:3%
want to post your password.
- Don't post it?

00:10:09.508 --> 00:10:11.344
- Don't have it on the back of
your computer written there,

00:10:11.344 --> 00:10:13.713
and then you take a selfie
and then everybody can see it

00:10:13.713 --> 00:10:14.981
'cause then the Bitcoin's gone.

00:10:14.981 --> 00:10:16.482
- I'm gonna have to
scribble that down.

00:10:16.482 --> 00:10:17.750
All right, I want to talk to
you about one other thing,

00:10:17.750 --> 00:10:19.952
which is that you basically

00:10:19.952 --> 00:10:22.655 align:start size:81% position:19%
were the first
big guy in Locals.

00:10:22.655 --> 00:10:24.557
You helped us put this
whole thing together

00:10:24.557 --> 00:10:26.826
and connected us with
a ton of investors.

00:10:26.826 --> 00:10:28.794 align:start size:84% position:16%
You opened up a
million doors for us.

00:10:28.794 --> 00:10:30.496
So I guess my question is

00:10:30.496 --> 00:10:32.732
what did you see in
what we were doing

00:10:32.732 --> 00:10:34.600
because we talked to a lot
of people at the beginning.

00:10:34.600 --> 00:10:35.901 align:start size:84% position:16%
A lot of people said,
it's a good idea,

00:10:35.901 --> 00:10:38.304
but you know it could
be a little dangerous

00:10:38.304 --> 00:10:40.306
and freeing people and all that.

00:10:40.306 --> 00:10:42.341 align:start size:97% position:3%
It made some people scared but
you were like, let's do it.

00:10:42.341 --> 00:10:43.676
- Well, I have to admit,

00:10:43.676 --> 00:10:45.778
the reason I took
a meeting with you

00:10:45.778 --> 00:10:47.780
is because I was a
fan of your work.

00:10:47.780 --> 00:10:49.315
So I like the way you think

00:10:49.315 --> 00:10:51.550
and I was like, well,
how bad can it be?

00:10:51.550 --> 00:10:53.653
I can meet this guy and it'll
be interesting conversation.

00:10:53.653 --> 00:10:55.087
But then when you described it

00:10:55.087 --> 00:10:57.490 align:start size:88% position:13%
in terms of giving power
back to the creator

00:10:58.524 --> 00:11:01.127 line:0%
and having a system where it's,

00:11:02.561 --> 00:11:05.765 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
I think, unfortunately, a
lot of times in our society,

00:11:05.765 --> 00:11:07.199
there's big tech companies

00:11:07.199 --> 00:11:08.901 align:start size:94% position:6%
that have gotten a little
bit too much power.

00:11:08.901 --> 00:11:10.436
And I really liked your

00:11:10.436 --> 00:11:14.373 align:start size:97% position:3%
David versus Google
Goliath approach to things.

00:11:14.373 --> 00:11:16.776 align:start size:94% position:6%
And when I make a decision
to invest in a company

00:11:16.776 --> 00:11:18.244
it's really about the team

00:11:18.244 --> 00:11:19.879 align:start size:97% position:3%
and your team with this
offer was really impressive

00:11:19.879 --> 00:11:23.115 align:start size:81% position:19%
and the technology
we're building

00:11:23.115 --> 00:11:24.784
was right on what I
thought could work.

00:11:24.784 --> 00:11:26.986 align:start size:84% position:16%
And then it's proven
really to be true

00:11:26.986 --> 00:11:30.523 align:start position:0%
and I have seen so many creators
thrive on your platform.

00:11:30.523 --> 00:11:33.325
They can quit their day
job and just be on Locals

00:11:33.325 --> 00:11:36.262
and have an amazing life
and just continue to create

00:11:36.262 --> 00:11:38.464
and that's impossible
on other platforms.

00:11:38.464 --> 00:11:41.200
So, I think I've
been proven right

00:11:41.200 --> 00:11:42.735
that investing in
you and your team

00:11:42.735 --> 00:11:44.036
has been a really good thing.

00:11:44.036 --> 00:11:46.272
- What's the funnest
part of doing this,

00:11:46.272 --> 00:11:49.375
watching these companies grow
and you've had some nice wins?

00:11:49.375 --> 00:11:50.776
- I love entrepreneurs.

00:11:50.776 --> 00:11:52.545
I think they're all
a little bit crazy,

00:11:52.545 --> 00:11:53.946
they take these crazy risks

00:11:53.946 --> 00:11:56.115 align:start size:88% position:13%
and it's really amazing
to see it pay off.

00:11:58.818 --> 00:12:00.720
No sane person wants
to be an entrepreneur

00:12:00.720 --> 00:12:02.188
but it's that good kind of crazy

00:12:02.188 --> 00:12:06.559 align:start position:0%
taking on these intractable
problems and building something

00:12:06.559 --> 00:12:09.328
and, to me, that's the most
fun, it's the human aspect.

00:12:09.328 --> 00:12:11.530
And I am kind of
a computer geek,

00:12:11.530 --> 00:12:14.433 align:start size:94% position:6%
I love all the
technology aspects of it.

00:12:14.433 --> 00:12:16.502
I'm a science fiction fan

00:12:16.502 --> 00:12:18.971
and I love the idea of seeing

00:12:18.971 --> 00:12:20.940
how Sci-Fi will play
out in the real world

00:12:20.940 --> 00:12:22.975
and what are the trends
that are going to work,

00:12:22.975 --> 00:12:25.444
have impact on society?

00:12:25.444 --> 00:12:27.646 align:start size:97% position:3%
- We're basically in a
dystopian future right now.

00:12:27.646 --> 00:12:29.715
- Sometimes I think that
we live in a simulation

00:12:29.715 --> 00:12:32.084
because craziest
stuff is happening.

00:12:32.084 --> 00:12:34.820 align:start size:88% position:13%
- So my last question
for you, we're in Miami.

00:12:34.820 --> 00:12:36.088
You moved to Miami,

00:12:36.088 --> 00:12:38.691
so you're a case of you
lived in San Francisco.

00:12:38.691 --> 00:12:39.692 align:start size:84% position:16%
How long did you live
in San Francisco?

00:12:39.692 --> 00:12:41.193
- 23 years.

00:12:41.193 --> 00:12:43.662
- Yeah, 23 years, you just
moved here in the last year.

00:12:43.662 --> 00:12:47.533 align:start size:94% position:6%
Beautiful wife, beautiful
kids, thriving here.

00:12:47.533 --> 00:12:49.401 align:start size:84% position:16%
You think I'm nuts
we're not leaving yet?

00:12:49.401 --> 00:12:51.804
- You know, take your time,
Miami will still be here

00:12:51.804 --> 00:12:54.707
but definitely move
here before your IPO.

00:12:56.575 --> 00:13:00.212
- True wisdom from a great
man and a great investor,

00:13:00.212 --> 00:13:01.013
Alex Lloyd.

00:13:01.013 --> 00:13:01.947
Thanks a lot, man.

